(175,"2026-08-08","User","Question","Is it true more than 50% of companies are beating results, and what happens next",
 "User heard that over half of companies reported better results and asked what follows",
 "DONE","2026-08-08",
 "ANSWERED, WITH THE SAMPLE LIMIT STATED UP FRONT. The premise needed correcting: a >50% beat rate is not a signal, it is the STRUCTURAL BASELINE - US beat rates run ~70-80% every quarter because guidance is walked down ahead of the print. So the informative questions are whether the rate is high or low VERSUS ITS OWN HISTORY, how big the beats are, and above all how prices REACT, since stocks routinely fall on a beat. This project already has the relevant machinery: /earnings, the PEAD post-earnings-drift scanner and a Finnhub key, so the answer was measured from our own data. MEASURED (earnings_history, 72 prints): beat rate 72.2%, median surprise +6.8% - consistent with the known ~70-80% structural baseline, confirming that 'over half beat' describes the normal state of the world rather than this quarter. HARD LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN BURIED: those 72 prints are only THREE tickers (AMD, GOOG, TSLA) over 2020-2026, so the number corroborates the structure but is NOT a market-wide measurement and was not presented as one. THE PART THAT MATTERS - the reaction: beating does not mean rising. 46% of stocks that BEAT still fell the next day, near a coin flip. Beats gained +1.15% next day, misses lost -4.17% (difference +5.33pp, t=+2.74, p=0.009). So the edge is not in the beat, it is in the ASYMMETRY: a miss is punished about four times harder than a beat is rewarded, which is what actually matters for anyone short premium into a print",
 "-","P1","GAP FOUND: earnings_history covers only 3 tickers. Widening the capture would make this answerable market-wide"),
(176,"2026-08-08","User","Bug","Big-move alert gives no confidence; add how often this happened before and what followed",
 "User: this is not giving me any confidence advise, can you make it more efficient. Also add stats - how many times in the past we had this scenario and what happened next",
 "DONE","2026-08-08",
 "THE USER WAS RIGHT, AND MEASURING IT FOUND A BROKEN SIGNAL. The rule was fires>=5, which fired on 134 OF 151 DAYS - 89% of the time. A warning that is on almost every day carries no information, and the week after it was statistically indistinguishable from any other week (SPY median |5d| 1.18% vs 1.18% baseline, Mann-Whitney p=0.243). So the vagueness was not a wording problem, it was an uncalibrated threshold - the same error as reading a dealers raw net instead of its percentile. Re-tested properly and found the effect IS real but had been mislabelled: it is QQQ-SPECIFIC and SIZE-ONLY. Put-flow vs forward |move|: QQQ 3d +0.213 p=0.008, 5d +0.315 p=0.00008, 10d +0.240 p=0.004; SPY 5d +0.112 p=0.17 and IWM 5d -0.096 p=0.24, i.e. nothing. Three adjacent horizons on QQQ all positive is a coherent effect rather than a multiple-testing artifact, and signed direction is ~0 everywhere (5d rho +0.015, p=0.85), which is exactly the projects existing put-flow-predicts-SIZE-not-DIRECTION finding. Rewritten: thresholds are now the 60th/80th percentile of the signals own history (13/17 fires) instead of an absolute 5, and the alert now leads with the BASE RATE the user asked for - after a HIGH reading a >2% Nasdaq week followed 62% of the time versus 38% after a normal day (median 2.26% vs 1.55%, p=0.012), with direction 53% up, still a coin flip. Verified live: today reads 19 fires = genuinely top-20%",
 "-","P1","Recalibrate the 13/17 thresholds if the universe or UOA rule changes"),
