 "ALL FOUR STORED, EACH TESTED INDIVIDUALLY - 2 of 4 actually work, and saying so is the point. Keys were written to plaintext api_keys.env one at a time, MERGED into the encrypted api_keys.enc, plaintext DELETED each time and verified absent; the merge was asserted non-clobbering (adding OpenRouter had to leave GROQ_API_KEY intact) and no key was ever echoed back or committed. RESULTS: GROQ PASS ~270ms - the workhorse. OPENROUTER PASS ~670ms but only after two fixes: the configured deepseek/deepseek-chat-v3-0324:free now returns 404 unavailable for free, as did deepseek-r1, llama-3.3-70b and qwen3-8b free slugs - only 14 slugs were genuinely free that day - so the default moved to nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free, found by listing the live catalogue rather than trusting a remembered name. GOOGLE FAIL, HTTP 429 exceeded your current quota - the key authenticates but the project carries no free-tier quota, so it is a Google-side plan issue, not our code. CEREBRAS FAIL, HTTP 402 Payment required - the key is valid and its three models are visible (gpt-oss-120b, gemma-4-31b, zai-glm-4.7) but the account needs billing enabled; note my llama-3.3-70b default 404d first because Cerebras model access is PER-ORGANISATION. Also fixed earlier in the same lane: Groq sits behind Cloudflare and 403d error 1010 on urllibs default User-Agent, the exact opposite of FRED in this file which requires it",
